<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Lindos</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 408 BCE (approx.) · capital Lindos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The greatest of the three old cities of Rhodes, crowned by the sanctuary of Athena Lindia and ruled by the sage Cleobulus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorian Greeks</a> settled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, forming three cities, Lindos, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ialysos">Ialysos</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kamiros">Kamiros</a>. Lindos, on the east coast with the island's best natural harbours, grew around its acropolis sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorian-hexapolis">Dorian Hexapolis</a> with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/knidos">Knidos</a>, Kos and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/halicarnassus">Halicarnassus</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lindian sailors founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gela">Gela</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> with Cretans in 688 BCE and traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. In the 6th century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleobulus">Cleobulus</a>, counted among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-sages-of-greece">Seven Sages</a>, ruled as tyrant and rebuilt the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>. Lindos later fell under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and then belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 408 BCE Lindos joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ialysos">Ialysos</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kamiros">Kamiros</a> to found the new city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, which became the island's capital. Lindos remained a religious centre: the Lindian Chronicle of 99 BCE lists famous offerings to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, and the acropolis is still a landmark.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lindos.jpg" alt="Lindos" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Berwing · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20210826-Lindos-DJI_0205.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 408 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lindos</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cleobulus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Athena Lindia</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-654&amp;focus=lindos" aria-label="Lindos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5388 875 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lindos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5708 1131L5591 1068M5708 1131L5703 1128"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5591 1068h0M5703 1128h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5708 1131h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5708 1131h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-654&amp;focus=lindos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
